\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The Active Inference symbols a GNN specification carries, each bound to the equation of sec.~\ref {sec:system_context} that fixes its role. Read \texttt {A}--\texttt {E} as the generative-model tensors (likelihood, controlled transitions, log-preferences, prior over initial states, habit prior), \texttt {s}, \texttt {o}, \texttt {\ensuremath  {\mitpi }}, and \texttt {u} as the inference variables, and \texttt {F} and \texttt {G} as the two free-energy functionals minimized at eq.~\ref {eq:vfe} and eq.~\ref {eq:efe}. Use the table as a decoding key when reading a \texttt {StateSpaceBlock}: every declared matrix name should map to exactly one row here, and the \breaktt {\#\#\ ActInfOntologyAnnotation} bindings described below are what make that mapping machine-checkable downstream.}}{22}{table.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tbl:actinf_symbols}{{5}{22}{The Active Inference symbols a GNN specification carries, each bound to the equation of sec.~\ref {sec:system_context} that fixes its role. Read \protect \texttt {A}--\protect \texttt {E} as the generative-model tensors (likelihood, controlled transitions, log-preferences, prior over initial states, habit prior), \protect \texttt {s}, \protect \texttt {o}, \protect \texttt {\texorpdfstring {\ensuremath {\pi }}{pi}}, and \protect \texttt {u} as the inference variables, and \protect \texttt {F} and \protect \texttt {G} as the two free-energy functionals minimized at eq.~\ref {eq:vfe} and eq.~\ref {eq:efe}. Use the table as a decoding key when reading a \protect \texttt {StateSpaceBlock}: every declared matrix name should map to exactly one row here, and the \protect \breaktt {\#\#\ ActInfOntologyAnnotation} bindings described below are what make that mapping machine-checkable downstream}{table.5}{}}
